Evaluating Test-Time Scaling of General LLM Agents
section: The Plateau of Sequential Scaling (other)

Sequential scaling allocates more test-time compute by extending the interaction turns. As the agent engages with the environment, the conversation grows. When the agent attempts to terminate an episode, we can replace the termination action with an additional round of instruction to encourage further reasoning. This is our main method used in the following experiments. More complex sequential scaling strategies include summarization [Park et al., 2023], verifier-guided revision [Zhu et al., 2025], and recent discarding and trajectory folding [Ye et al., 2025]. We conduct ablations on the choice of strategy in Section 4.3. We first investigate whether sequential scaling continues to yield meaningful improvement and, if not, when it hits its upper bound. We pre-select several context budgets depending on the task domain. This token budget is defined as the accumulated input context length for the next turn, including all previously generated text and tool results / environmental feedback. For each budget, the agent starts fresh from the question-rather than continuing from a shorter run-and is forced to keep working until reaching the predefined budget. Figure 3 plots each agent's accuracy against the context budget. We observe that (1) most agents utilize additional compute effectively at first, but after a certain point performance saturates or even degrades;

