We describe the world rehearsal formulation and the role-wise optimization objective in Section 4 with the complete training procedure, and report the datasets, backbone models, baselines, and evaluation protocol of every benchmark in Section 5.1. All benchmarks are public and we follow each benchmark's official evaluation protocol without modification, so that reported numbers are directly comparable to previously published results. Full training and evaluation hyperparameters, including the learning rate, rollout configuration, sequence lengths, and the maximum number of interaction turns, are reported in Section 5.1, together with qualitative rehearsal trajectories in Appendix A that illustrate the exact prompt format used for the actor and the environment roles. Our code, prompts, and configuration files are released at the anonymized repository linked in the abstract.
As shown in Figure 8, this case illustrates how EnvACE avoids an invalid write operation through world rehearsal. The user requests to modify a flight reservation with a basic economy ticket. Although updating the reservation appears to be a reasonable next step, such a write operation is not allowed under the given environment constraints. Before execution, EnvACE performs internal world rehearsal by simulating the planned tool call and predicting its potential response. It recognizes that the required information for executing update reservation flights is incomplete and that issuing the write operation would lead to an invalid action. Therefore, EnvACE proactively replaces the unsafe action with a read-only query to obtain reservation details, avoiding any forbidden modification. In contrast, EnvScaler8B and Vanilla agents fail to anticipate the consequence of tool calls and eventually execute the invalid write operation, resulting in failure. This example demonstrates that world rehearsal enables EnvACE to anticipate tool consequences and make safer decisions before interacting with the external environment.
